window.RMSStudentGuidance={"version":"2.1","glossary":{"analysis":"The process used to organize evidence and answer the research question. Analysis can be descriptive, statistical, qualitative, or a synthesis of existing studies.","association":"A pattern in which two variables vary together. An association by itself does not show that one variable caused the other.","attrition":"Loss of participants or units after a study begins. If attrition differs across conditions or types of participants, it can change the interpretation.","binary":"A variable with exactly two possible categories for the analysis, such as germinated/not germinated, correct/incorrect, or yes/no. Binary describes the number of categories, not whether the variable is numerical.","blinding":"Keeping a person from knowing information that could influence behavior, measurement, or scoring, such as which treatment a sample received.","blocking":"Grouping similar experimental units before random assignment, then assigning conditions within those groups. This can reduce imbalance on an important characteristic.","boolean search":"A database search built from concepts connected with operators such as AND and OR. OR usually joins synonyms; AND usually joins different concepts.","calibration":"Checking or adjusting a measurement instrument against a known reference so its readings can be interpreted consistently.","categorical":"Data that place observations into named groups or categories. Examples include habitat type, treatment group, blood type, and species. Arithmetic on the category names is usually meaningless.","causal claim":"A claim that changing one factor produces a change in an outcome. Strong causal claims need a design that addresses alternative explanations, not merely a statistically significant result.","chi-square test":"A test commonly used with categorical count data to examine whether two categorical variables are associated. It uses counts in cells, not percentages treated as if they were raw observations.","coding":"Applying explicit labels or rules to data. In qualitative research it can mean labeling sections of text; in quantitative data it can mean defining how categories such as yes/no are stored.","confidence interval":"A range calculated from the data and statistical model that communicates uncertainty around an estimate. It should be interpreted with the design and assumptions, not as a guarantee that a parameter lies inside the interval.","confounder":"A factor related to both the exposure/comparison and the outcome that can create, hide, or distort an observed relationship.","construct":"An idea you want to study that is not yet a direct measurement, such as stress, memory, biodiversity, engagement, or trust.","continuous":"A numerical variable that can, in principle, take many values across an interval, such as temperature, mass, time, or concentration. Real instruments may record it to limited precision.","control condition":"A comparison condition used as a reference. It is different from controlled conditions, which are procedures or environmental features kept similar.","controlled condition":"A feature deliberately standardized across study conditions so it is less likely to explain a difference. Examples include the same measurement time, volume, or scoring procedure.","correlation":"A numerical description of the direction and strength of association between two variables. Correlation does not by itself establish causation.","counterbalancing":"Changing the order of conditions across units so that one condition is not always experienced first. It helps address order, practice, fatigue, and carryover effects.","covariate":"A measured variable included because it may explain outcome variation, improve precision, or address a known source of imbalance. Whether statistical adjustment is appropriate depends on the design and causal question.","data dictionary":"A document that explains every dataset column, what it means, its type, units or categories, coding rules, and how missing values are recorded.","dependent variable":"A common experimental term for the measured outcome expected to respond to the manipulated independent variable.","descriptive statistics":"Numerical summaries of observed data, such as mean, median, standard deviation, interquartile range, counts, and proportions.","effect size":"A quantitative description of the magnitude of a difference or association. A small p-value does not automatically mean a large effect.","estimand":"The exact quantity the study is trying to estimate. Examples include a mean difference, a paired mean change, a proportion, a correlation, or a risk difference.","experimental unit":"The smallest unit that could independently receive a different treatment condition. Measurements or subsamples taken from one experimental unit do not automatically become new experimental units.","exposure":"A measured condition, behavior, characteristic, or environmental factor that was observed rather than assigned by the researcher.","external validity":"How well a study's conclusions may apply beyond the specific units, setting, measurements, and conditions studied.","Fisher exact test":"An exact test for association in a 2\u00d72 categorical table. It is often considered when counts are sparse and chi-square approximations may be questionable.","generalization":"Extending a conclusion from the studied sample or setting to a broader population or context. The sampling and study context determine how far this can reasonably go.","hypothesis":"A specific prediction that can be evaluated with evidence. A formal hypothesis is useful for many confirmatory quantitative studies but is not required for every research design.","identifier":"A value used to keep track of a unit, such as plant_01 or participant_A17. An identifier helps preserve pairing and nesting without being an outcome itself.","independent groups":"Groups containing different independent units. A person, plant, classroom, or sample should not appear in more than one independent group unless the design explicitly has repeated measurements.","independent variable":"In an experiment, the factor deliberately manipulated by the researcher. A measured predictor in an observational study should usually be called a predictor or exposure, not a manipulated independent variable.","independence":"A statistical/design condition concerning whether observations provide separate information. Repeated measurements from the same unit, students in one classroom, or subsamples from one specimen are often dependent.","inference":"A conclusion that goes beyond simply listing the observed data. Statistical inference uses a model and assumptions; scientific inference also depends on the research design.","interquartile range":"The distance between the 25th and 75th percentiles. It summarizes the spread of the middle half of numerical data and is often abbreviated IQR.","interval estimate":"An estimate reported as a range to communicate uncertainty, such as a confidence interval.","literature gap":"A defensible description of something the existing literature has not adequately addressed. A gap should come from a sufficiently broad, documented search and synthesis, not from failing to find a paper quickly.","mean":"The arithmetic average. It is useful for many numerical variables, but it can be influenced by extreme values and may not describe a strongly skewed distribution well by itself.","measurement validity":"Evidence supporting the interpretation you want to make from a measurement or score. A reliable measure can still measure the wrong thing.","median":"The middle value after numerical observations are ordered. It can be useful for skewed distributions or when extreme values strongly affect the mean.","meta-analysis":"A quantitative synthesis that combines comparable effect estimates from multiple studies using an explicit statistical model.","missing data":"A value that was not observed or recorded. The reason for missingness can matter; blank cells should not silently mean multiple different things.","moderator":"A variable associated with a difference in the size or direction of a relationship across groups or conditions.","nominal":"Categorical data with no inherent ranking, such as species, blood type, or classroom section.","null hypothesis":"A formal statistical hypothesis used by an inferential test, often representing no difference, no association, or a specified parameter value under the model. It is not automatically the researcher's true scientific belief.","numerical":"Data expressed as meaningful quantities for which arithmetic operations make sense, such as height, time, concentration, or score.","observational study":"A study in which the researcher measures variables or exposures without assigning the key exposure of interest.","observational unit":"The unit on which variables are observed or recorded. It may or may not be the same as the experimental unit in an experiment.","operational definition":"The exact rule for how a construct will be manipulated, measured, counted, scored, coded, or observed.","ordinal":"Categories with a meaningful order but without guaranteed equal spacing between levels, such as low/medium/high or a 1\u20135 agreement rating.","outcome":"The measured response the research question is trying to explain, compare, describe, or predict.","outlier":"An observation unusually far from the rest of the data under a chosen diagnostic rule. An outlier flag is a reason to investigate, not an automatic instruction to delete the value.","paired data":"Two related observations that must stay linked, usually because they come from the same unit measured twice or from deliberately matched units.","parameter":"A quantity describing a population or statistical model, such as a population mean or correlation. A sample estimate is used to learn about it.","p-value":"Under a specified null model and assumptions, the probability of obtaining data at least as incompatible with that model as the observed result. It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true, and it does not measure effect size.","population":"The broader group or system the research question refers to. The actual sample is the smaller set that is studied.","predictor":"A measured variable used to explain or predict variation in an outcome. In observational research it is often clearer than calling the variable an independent variable.","protocol":"The planned research procedure, including design, measurements, data handling, and decision rules. Locking a protocol preserves what was planned before seeing the final results.","pseudoreplication":"Treating repeated measurements, subsamples, or clustered observations as if each were an independent treatment replicate when they are not.","qualitative data":"Non-numerical evidence such as interview transcripts, written responses, field notes, or observations used to understand meaning, experience, process, or explanation.","random assignment":"Using a random process to place experimental units into conditions. It helps make treatment groups comparable for causal inference.","random sampling":"Using a probability process to select units from a defined population or sampling frame. It addresses population representation, which is a different problem from treatment assignment.","reliability":"Consistency or repeatability of measurement under relevant conditions. Reliability does not guarantee that the measure captures the intended construct.","repeated measures":"Multiple measurements from the same unit across times, trials, or conditions. The repeated observations are related and should not usually be treated as independent.","replication":"Independent repetition that provides evidence about variability across independent units. Repeated readings of the same sample are not automatically independent replication.","research question":"The exact question the study will answer with evidence. Its wording should match the design and the claim that can be supported.","sample":"The units actually studied.","sampling frame":"The list or practical mechanism from which a probability sample is selected.","sampling method":"The process used to decide which units enter the sample, such as convenience, purposive, simple random, or stratified sampling.","scale":"The form in which a measurement is expressed, including physical units, score ranges, ordered categories, or coding categories.","standard deviation":"A numerical summary of spread around the mean. It is meaningful for numerical data and is abbreviated SD.","statistical significance":"A decision label based on a prespecified statistical rule, often involving a p-value threshold. It does not automatically mean the effect is large, important, causal, or reproducible.","stratification":"Dividing units into meaningful groups before sampling or assignment so those groups are represented or balanced deliberately.","subsample":"A smaller observation taken from within a larger unit, such as several leaves measured from one plant. Subsamples improve information about that unit but do not automatically increase independent replication.","synthesis":"Combining evidence across multiple sources to describe patterns, disagreements, explanations, and boundaries rather than listing each source separately.","technical replicate":"Repeated measurement or processing of the same underlying sample to assess technical variation. It is not the same as an independently treated experimental replicate.","theme":"A meaningful pattern used to organize qualitative evidence or literature synthesis. A theme should be supported by traceable evidence, not merely chosen because it sounds important.","TRAPP":"A source-evaluation framework using Timeframe, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy, and Purpose.","variable":"A characteristic that can take different values across observations, units, times, or conditions.","within-unit design":"A design in which the same unit contributes data under multiple conditions or times. It creates paired or repeated data.","Welch t-test":"A two-independent-group t-test for comparing means that does not require the two population variances to be equal.","ANOVA":"Analysis of variance. A family of methods used to compare mean structure across multiple groups or conditions. The exact ANOVA model must match independence and repeated-measure structure.","McNemar test":"A test for change or difference in a binary outcome measured twice on the same units. It focuses on units that changed category.","Cochran Q":"An omnibus test for a binary outcome measured under three or more related conditions on the same units.","regression":"A statistical model describing how an outcome changes with one or more predictors. The appropriate model depends on the outcome type, design, and assumptions.","citation":"A reference in the text that points to a source. Citation integrity requires the source to exist and actually support the nearby claim.","identifiable data":"Information that directly identifies a person or can reasonably be linked back to them using available information."},"stages":{"1":{"title":"Start with curiosity, then make it researchable","plain":"You are not choosing a statistical test or writing a perfect research question yet. You are collecting possible things worth investigating.","steps":["Write several topics, observations, problems, or claims that genuinely make you curious.","For each idea, ask what evidence could possibly help you learn more about it.","Notice what you can realistically access: materials, organisms, public datasets, school settings, articles, or safe observations.","Generate at least three directions before choosing one. The goal is to avoid locking onto the first idea just because it appeared first."],"strong":"A strong Stage 1 record gives several possible directions and connects curiosity to a possible source of evidence. It can still be messy.","examples":[{"label":"Biology","text":"Interest: plant stress. Observation: some houseplants near a sunny window wilt faster. Possible direction: compare soil moisture loss under controlled light/temperature conditions."},{"label":"Learning","text":"Interest: memory. Observation: vocabulary feels easier to remember after self-testing. Possible direction: compare delayed recall after two study procedures."},{"label":"Literature-based","text":"Interest: microplastics. Question I keep seeing: how consistently are microplastics measured across freshwater studies? Possible direction: literature review of sampling and measurement methods."}],"stuck":["List a science topic you would voluntarily watch a video about.","Write one sentence beginning with \u201cI have noticed that\u2026\u201d","Write one claim beginning with \u201cPeople often say that\u2026\u201d and ask what evidence would be needed to evaluate it.","Choose something you can safely observe or measure with the time and resources you actually have."],"terms":["variable","construct","qualitative data"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student is curious about why some plants in the school courtyard wilt faster.","thinking":["I should not jump straight to 'sun causes wilting.'","I can list several possibilities: light, soil moisture, pot size, watering schedule.","I can access plants and environmental measurements, but I cannot manipulate school landscaping without permission."],"draft":"Possible direction: observe or experimentally model how light exposure relates to soil moisture loss using standardized containers."}},"2":{"title":"Shrink the idea until one student project can actually do it","plain":"A topic is still broader than a research question. Here you define the boundaries of the project so it can be completed safely and well.","steps":["Name the broad area, then one narrower subtopic.","Specify what phenomenon, relationship, experience, or problem you care about.","Specify the population, organism, system, setting, or context.","Identify what evidence you could realistically obtain.","Cut away parts that require too much time, unsafe procedures, inaccessible equipment, or authority you do not have."],"strong":"A strong topic is specific enough that you can imagine the evidence you would collect or review, while still leaving room to refine the exact research question.","examples":[{"label":"Too broad \u2192 workable","text":"Climate change \u2192 urban heat \u2192 temperature differences among shaded and unshaded school surfaces during a defined time window."},{"label":"Too broad \u2192 workable","text":"Social media \u2192 short-form video use \u2192 association between self-reported evening use and bedtime in one accessible student sample."}],"stuck":["Add a location or context.","Add a specific population or biological system.","Add one measurable or observable feature.","Ask what part could be completed in the course timeline."],"terms":["population","sample","generalization","observational study"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The student's original topic is 'climate change.'","thinking":["That topic is far too large for one class project.","I can focus on one local phenomenon that is measurable.","Surface temperature in shaded and unshaded school areas is feasible during a defined afternoon period."],"draft":"Working topic: afternoon surface-temperature differences between shaded and unshaded locations on the school campus."}},"3":{"title":"Read enough background to improve the question before committing","plain":"This is a preliminary scan. You are learning the vocabulary, common methods, and what researchers already know so you do not build a question on a misunderstanding.","steps":["Write the main concepts in your possible topic.","List synonyms, scientific terms, and related phrases researchers might use.","Try several preliminary searches.","Record what appears well established and what still seems uncertain.","Notice the methods and measurements researchers actually use.","Revise your possible question if the literature changes your understanding."],"strong":"A strong background scan changes or sharpens your thinking. It does not pretend to be a complete literature review.","examples":[{"label":"Search vocabulary","text":"Everyday term: studying. Research terms might include retrieval practice, practice testing, rereading, delayed recall, retention."},{"label":"Question shift","text":"Before reading: Does sleep improve grades? After reading: Is self-reported weekday sleep duration associated with current course grade in this sample?"}],"stuck":["Search the broad concept plus \u201creview\u201d to learn field vocabulary.","Look at keywords used by two relevant papers.","Record methods even when the paper does not answer your exact question."],"terms":["construct","operational definition","predictor","outcome"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The student initially searches 'studying better.'","thinking":["The phrase is everyday language, not field vocabulary.","Papers use retrieval practice, practice testing, retention, delayed recall, and feedback.","Those terms help me write a more precise question."],"draft":"Question shift: from 'Does testing help studying?' to a question about assigned retrieval practice and delayed recall."}},"4":{"title":"Write a question that your evidence can actually answer","plain":"The research question is the central contract of the study. It tells you what evidence is needed and limits what the final conclusion can claim.","steps":["Decide whether you mainly want to describe, examine a relationship, test a possible causal effect, understand experiences/processes, or synthesize literature.","Name the key variables/constructs or phenomenon.","Name the population/system/context when it matters.","Use wording that matches the design you can realistically use.","Check that the answer could come from evidence, not opinion.","Write several versions and choose the clearest."],"strong":"A strong question is clear, bounded, answerable, feasible, ethical, and uses causal language only when the planned design can support it.","examples":[{"label":"Descriptive","text":"What proportion of sampled seedlings germinate under each salinity condition during seven days?"},{"label":"Observational","text":"What is the association between reported weekday sleep duration and biology quiz score among students in this class?"},{"label":"Experimental","text":"How does assigned salinity level affect mean seedling growth under standardized laboratory conditions?"},{"label":"Qualitative","text":"How do students describe the reasons they hesitate to ask questions during science lessons?"}],"stuck":["Start with \u201cWhat is\u2026\u201d for description.","Start with \u201cWhat is the relationship between\u2026\u201d for observational association.","Use \u201cHow does assigned/manipulated X affect Y?\u201d only for a genuine intervention design.","Start with \u201cHow do participants describe\u2026\u201d for experience-focused qualitative work."],"terms":["research question","association","causal claim","predictor","outcome"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student wants to ask 'Does phone use lower grades?'","thinking":["I will only measure existing phone use, not assign it.","That means the design is observational.","My question should ask about association, not whether phone use causes lower grades."],"draft":"What is the association between self-reported daily phone use and current course grade in this sample?"}},"5":{"title":"Build a search that another person could understand and repeat","plain":"A literature search is more than typing one sentence into Google. You break the question into concepts, add synonyms, and document where and how you searched.","steps":["Identify two or three concept blocks from the question.","Add synonyms, spelling variants, scientific terms, and related phrases within each block.","Join synonyms with OR and different concepts with AND.","Choose appropriate databases or scholarly search tools.","Set inclusion/exclusion criteria before screening results.","Record the exact search string, date, filters, and what you changed."],"strong":"A strong search is broad enough to find relevant studies, specific enough to screen efficiently, and documented so your gap claims can be evaluated.","examples":[{"label":"Boolean structure","text":"(\"retrieval practice\" OR \"practice testing\") AND (biology OR science) AND (retention OR recall)"},{"label":"Concept blocks","text":"Block A: microplastic OR microplastics. Block B: freshwater OR river OR lake. Block C: sampling OR detection OR measurement."}],"stuck":["Copy the main nouns from your research question.","For each noun, ask what another researcher might call the same idea.","Do not add every possible concept with AND; over-constraining a search can hide relevant papers."],"terms":["boolean search","sampling frame","literature gap"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The question concerns retrieval practice in biology learning.","thinking":["My main concepts are retrieval practice, biology/science learning, and delayed retention.","I need synonyms within each concept block.","OR connects synonyms; AND connects different concepts."],"draft":"(\"retrieval practice\" OR \"practice testing\") AND (biology OR science) AND (retention OR recall)"}},"6":{"title":"Turn each source into an evidence record you can use later","plain":"Do not only save a PDF. Extract the information that will let you compare studies and verify later claims.","steps":["Record complete bibliographic information and verify it against the actual source.","Record design, sample/context, variables or constructs, measures, and key findings.","Record important limitations.","Evaluate the source using TRAPP.","Mark whether the source is included, pending, or excluded and why.","Separate what the authors measured, what they observed, and how they interpreted it."],"strong":"A strong source record makes later synthesis possible without rereading the entire paper to remember basic details.","examples":[{"label":"Useful extraction","text":"Design: cross-sectional observational. Sample: 420 secondary students. Measure: self-reported activity hours + validated well-being scale. Finding: small positive association. Limits: self-report, cross-sectional, possible confounding."}],"stuck":["Start with Methods to find design/sample/measures.","Use Results for the observed evidence.","Use Discussion to record the authors' interpretation and limitations, but do not confuse interpretation with the result itself."],"terms":["TRAPP","observational study","measurement validity","confounder"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A paper reports a strong result but uses one university course and self-reported outcomes.","thinking":["The finding may still be useful, but I need to record the design and measure before citing it broadly.","Peer reviewed does not automatically mean the evidence fits my claim.","I should separate the observed result from the authors' interpretation."],"draft":"Include with quality note: limited context and self-report measurement; use only for claims matching that scope."}},"7":{"title":"Compare studies to find patterns, limits, and a defensible reason for your study","plain":"Synthesis asks what the collection of studies says. You are moving beyond one-paper-at-a-time notes.","steps":["Group studies by themes, methods, populations, measures, or findings.","Identify where several studies agree.","Identify where results differ and look for design/context explanations.","Track repeated limitations.","Choose a study rationale that the evidence actually supports: replication, extension, inconsistency, method comparison, local question, or a documented gap.","Write a bounded gap/rationale statement."],"strong":"A strong synthesis names both patterns and boundaries and does not claim global novelty from a small search.","examples":[{"label":"Synthesis","text":"Across three studies, retrieval practice was associated with stronger delayed retention, although effect magnitude varied with feedback timing and retention interval."},{"label":"Bounded rationale","text":"Existing studies largely use university samples; this project is a local classroom extension in a secondary biology context, not evidence that adolescent research is absent globally."}],"stuck":["Put two studies side by side and ask what is the same and what differs.","Compare measures before comparing findings.","If you cannot prove a global gap, use replication, extension, or local relevance as a legitimate rationale."],"terms":["synthesis","theme","literature gap","generalization"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"Three studies mostly agree, but one reports a smaller effect.","thinking":["I should not label the fourth study an outlier and ignore it.","The studies use different feedback timing and retention intervals.","The disagreement may reveal a boundary condition instead of a simple contradiction."],"draft":"Theme: retrieval practice generally supports delayed retention, with effect magnitude varying across feedback and retention conditions."}},"8":{"title":"Write about the literature as a conversation, not a stack of summaries","plain":"A literature-review paragraph should usually make a claim about several sources, compare them, qualify the claim, and show why the pattern matters.","steps":["Choose an organizing structure such as themes, methods, chronology, or theory.","Give each paragraph one clear job.","Begin with a claim about the literature, not automatically an author name.","Use multiple sources where the claim genuinely depends on multiple studies.","Explain agreements, differences, and possible reasons for them.","End the review by showing how the evidence leads to the study purpose/question."],"strong":"A strong review is traceable to sources and demonstrates relationships among studies.","examples":[{"label":"Weak","text":"Smith studied memory. Lee studied memory. Park studied memory."},{"label":"Stronger","text":"Across three studies, delayed recall improved after retrieval-based practice, although the size of the advantage was smaller when feedback was delayed (citations)."}],"stuck":["Write the paragraph's idea in one sentence before adding citations.","Ask \u201cWhat do these sources collectively support?\u201d","If a paragraph contains only one source because the claim truly is source-specific, make that purpose explicit."],"terms":["synthesis","theme","citation","literature gap"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The student's draft has one paragraph per article.","thinking":["Readers need a claim about the literature, not a reading log.","I can group the three articles around delayed retention, then compare where they differ.","Citations should support the claim after I make it."],"draft":"Across the included studies, retrieval-based practice generally improved delayed retention, although effect magnitude varied with feedback timing and test delay (citations)."}},"9":{"title":"Choose a design by asking what kind of answer the question needs","plain":"Research design is the overall evidence structure. Do not choose 'experiment' because it sounds more scientific.","steps":["Identify whether the question asks for description, association, causal effect, experience/process, mixed evidence, or literature synthesis.","Ask whether the key exposure/factor will be assigned by the researcher or only observed.","Ask whether the same unit is measured more than once.","Identify the strongest claim the design could reasonably support.","Choose the design family that matches those answers."],"strong":"A strong design choice explains why that design can answer the question and explicitly states what it cannot establish.","examples":[{"label":"Association","text":"Question: Is weekly study time associated with exam score? Design: observational correlational, because study time is measured, not assigned."},{"label":"Causal comparison","text":"Question: How does assigned retrieval practice versus rereading affect delayed recall? Design: experimental if units can be appropriately assigned and other validity protections are in place."}],"stuck":["If you do not assign the key exposure, start by thinking observational.","If you mainly want meanings or experiences, consider qualitative.","If all evidence comes from existing studies, consider a literature review or, only when comparable quantitative effects can be pooled appropriately, meta-analysis."],"terms":["observational study","independent variable","exposure","random assignment","causal claim"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student asks whether sleep causes better grades but cannot assign sleep duration.","thinking":["The factor is observed, not manipulated.","A correlational/observational design can estimate association.","Even a strong association would not establish causation."],"draft":"Design: observational correlational. Claim ceiling: association within the studied sample/context."}},"10":{"title":"Translate ideas into exact variables, measurements, units, and comparison roles","plain":"Terms such as stress, memory, growth, engagement, or pollution are ideas until you define exactly how they will appear in the study.","steps":["Decide whether a formal hypothesis is appropriate for this question.","Name each central construct or variable.","Give each variable a role that matches the design.","Write an operational definition: exactly what will be measured, counted, scored, coded, observed, or manipulated.","Identify the experimental or observational unit.","Separate control/comparison conditions from controlled conditions.","List potential alternative explanations or confounders."],"strong":"A strong plan lets another person know exactly what each variable means in this study and what unit provides independent evidence.","examples":[{"label":"Operational definition","text":"Memory retention \u2192 number of 20 target definitions recalled correctly 48 hours after study, scored with a prespecified answer key."},{"label":"Unit distinction","text":"If salt solution is assigned to whole dishes, the dish may be the experimental unit even when several seeds are measured inside each dish."}],"stuck":["For every abstract word, finish the sentence \u201cIn this study, I will know this by\u2026\u201d","Ask what object could independently receive a different treatment.","If you did not manipulate a variable, use predictor/exposure terminology when appropriate."],"terms":["construct","operational definition","experimental unit","confounder","control condition","controlled condition","hypothesis"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student says the independent variable is 'stress' and the dependent variable is 'performance.'","thinking":["Those are still constructs, not exact data.","If stress is observed, calling it a manipulated independent variable is misleading.","I need an operational definition for both constructs and the actual unit."],"draft":"Predictor/exposure: self-reported stress score from a specified scale. Outcome: number of correct items on the defined task. Unit: one participant."}},"11":{"title":"Decide who or what supplies evidence and whether the measurements deserve your interpretation","plain":"Sampling and measurement determine what the data represent. Ethics determines whether you are allowed to collect the data in the first place.","steps":["Define the target population/system and the actual sample.","Explain how units enter the sample.","State the limits on generalization.","Name the instrument or measurement procedure.","Explain reliability/consistency and why the measure is valid for the intended construct.","Identify privacy, safety, consent, authority, environmental, or other ethical issues.","Identify who must review/approve the plan."],"strong":"A strong plan connects sampling to generalization and measurement evidence to the exact interpretation you want to make.","examples":[{"label":"Sampling","text":"Target population: all students in the school. Actual sample: students in one available biology class. Sampling method: convenience. Generalization: conclusions should remain local to the studied class/context."},{"label":"Measurement","text":"Growth: change in stem height in centimeters, measured from soil surface to apical tip with the same ruler and procedure at each time point."}],"stuck":["Do not write 'random' unless a random process is actually used.","Ask who or what you want your conclusion to refer to, then ask who or what you can actually study.","For reliability ask whether the measurement is consistent; for validity ask whether the measurement supports the intended meaning."],"terms":["population","sample","sampling method","random sampling","random assignment","reliability","measurement validity","calibration","blinding"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student has access to one biology class and wants to generalize to all teenagers.","thinking":["Access does not make this a random sample.","The actual sample is one convenience class.","The result can be useful locally, but the generalization has to stay narrow."],"draft":"Sampling: convenience sample from one class. Generalization: limited to similar local context; no claim of national representativeness."}},"12":{"title":"Write the protocol and dataset structure before final data collection","plain":"This stage turns the design into an exact procedure and a data table. Decisions made here are easier to defend when they are made before seeing the final results.","steps":["List materials and preparation.","Write ordered procedure steps in enough detail for another person to reproduce the study.","State independent replication and repeated/subsample measurements separately.","Record randomization, counterbalancing, blinding, standardization, and other protections where applicable.","Define what one dataset row represents.","Define every column, its data type, units/categories, and missing-value code.","Decide missing-data and exclusion rules in advance where possible.","State the planned analysis at the level you can justify before data exist."],"strong":"A strong protocol links each research question component to a measurement and each measurement to a clearly structured dataset.","examples":[{"label":"Row structure","text":"One row = one plant measured at one time point. Columns include plant_id, dish_id, salinity, day, height_cm. Because rows repeat within plant, plant_id must be preserved."},{"label":"Binary column","text":"germinated: Binary. Allowed values 1 = visible radicle emerged, 0 = no visible radicle by observation time."}],"stuck":["Imagine handing your procedure and blank data table to another student. What would they still have to guess?","If one unit appears in several rows, include a stable ID.","Use explicit missing codes or documented blank rules; do not let blank mean several different things."],"terms":["protocol","replication","repeated measures","subsample","pseudoreplication","data dictionary","binary","categorical","ordinal","numerical"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"A student measures 20 seeds in one dish per treatment.","thinking":["Each seed gives a measurement, but the whole dish receives the treatment together.","Dish may be the experimental unit.","My data table needs dish_id and seed_id so I do not mistake 20 subsamples for 20 independent treatment units."],"draft":"One row = one seed measurement; include dish_id and seed_id. Independent treatment replication must come from multiple separately treated dishes."}},"13":{"title":"Protect the raw evidence, then understand what the dataset actually contains","plain":"Before formal statistics, verify data quality, structure, missingness, and patterns. Cleaning decisions must be documented instead of silently changing inconvenient values.","steps":["Preserve an unchanged raw-data file.","Check IDs, units, coding, impossible values, and exact duplicates.","Record missing observations and why they are missing when known.","Document corrections and protocol deviations.","Visualize numerical distributions and paired/repeated relationships.","Calculate appropriate descriptive summaries.","Keep the study's independent/repeated structure visible."],"strong":"A strong Stage 13 record can explain where the raw file is, what was changed in any cleaned file, what is missing, and what the data look like before inference.","examples":[{"label":"Numerical","text":"height_cm is numerical. Inspect distribution, minimum/maximum, mean/SD, and median/IQR when useful."},{"label":"Categorical/binary","text":"germinated is binary because each observation is in one of two categories. Summarize with counts and proportions."}],"stuck":["Start by asking what one row represents.","Check each column against the data dictionary.","Plot before testing.","Do not delete an unusual value until you investigate whether it is an error, a real extreme observation, or a protocol deviation."],"terms":["binary","categorical","numerical","ordinal","missing data","outlier","descriptive statistics","standard deviation","interquartile range","paired data"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"One plant height is much larger than the rest.","thinking":["An unusual value is not automatically an error.","First I check the source record and measurement notes.","If valid, I retain it and document sensitivity/diagnostic concerns instead of deleting it to improve results."],"draft":"Outlier flagged for review; source sheet confirms value is valid, so it remains in the dataset."}},"14":{"title":"Choose analysis from the question, design, unit structure, and outcome type","plain":"Do not start by memorizing test names. First identify the exact quantity you want to learn and the structure of the data.","steps":["State the estimand in ordinary language: mean difference, paired change, proportion, association, slope, theme, etc.","Identify the outcome type: numerical, count, ordinal, binary, nominal categorical, or text.","Identify whether groups are independent or the same units are repeated/paired.","Check whether observations are nested or clustered.","Choose a descriptive or inferential method that matches that structure.","Check relevant assumptions using plots and design information.","Plan to report magnitude and uncertainty, not only a p-value."],"strong":"A strong analysis plan can explain why the method answers the research question and why the data satisfy the structure the method expects.","examples":[{"label":"Binary","text":"Correct/incorrect measured before and after on the same students \u2192 paired binary data. A McNemar-type analysis preserves the pairing."},{"label":"Two independent numerical groups","text":"Different plants in fertilizer A and B, numerical growth outcome \u2192 compare group distributions and means; Welch's t-test can be appropriate when a mean contrast answers the question."},{"label":"Repeated binary 3+","text":"The same units measured yes/no under three conditions \u2192 repeated binary structure; Cochran's Q is one possible omnibus analysis."}],"stuck":["Ask 'same units or different units?'","Ask 'numbers, categories, ordered categories, two-category outcome, or text?'","Ask 'what exact difference/association/proportion do I want to estimate?'","Use the wizard only after answering those questions."],"terms":["estimand","numerical","categorical","binary","ordinal","nominal","paired data","independent groups","effect size","confidence interval","p-value","Welch t-test","ANOVA","McNemar test","Cochran Q"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The same students are coded correct/incorrect before and after an activity.","thinking":["The outcome has exactly two categories, so it is binary.","The same students appear twice, so the data are paired.","I need a paired binary analysis, not a test that treats the two proportions as independent groups."],"draft":"Structure: paired binary. Estimand: change in the proportion correct based on within-student category changes; consider McNemar-type analysis."}},"15":{"title":"Report the evidence clearly before explaining why it happened","plain":"The Results section tells the reader what the analysis produced. Interpretation, explanations, recommendations, and literature comparison usually belong in Discussion.","steps":["State the analyzed sample or corpus and any exclusions.","Report descriptive evidence first when useful.","Report the primary estimate or pattern.","Give uncertainty, test statistic, p-value, and effect size when appropriate to the method.","Report secondary and null/weak/unexpected results honestly.","Point readers to the exact table/figure/output supporting each statement.","Do not upgrade association to causation because a p-value is small."],"strong":"A strong Results section is traceable, complete, neutral, and includes weak or contradictory findings.","examples":[{"label":"Quantitative style","text":"Mean growth was 1.8 cm higher in group A than group B (95% CI ...), with the corresponding Welch test reported afterward."},{"label":"Boundary","text":"The variables were positively associated. This wording reports the observed relationship without claiming one caused the other."}],"stuck":["Write what happened numerically or thematically before writing why.","If you type 'because', check whether you have moved into Discussion.","For every number ask where it came from."],"terms":["effect size","confidence interval","p-value","statistical significance","inference"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"An observational study finds r = .52 with p < .01.","thinking":["I should report the association, coefficient, sample information, and uncertainty if available.","The small p-value does not turn the study into an experiment.","I should not explain the mechanism in Results."],"draft":"The variables were positively associated, r = .52, p < .01. Causal interpretation is reserved for Discussion and remains unsupported by this observational design."}},"16":{"title":"Explain what the results mean while keeping every claim inside the study's limits","plain":"Discussion connects evidence to meaning. It should answer the research question, explain important patterns, compare with prior research, and analyze limitations.","steps":["Give a direct answer to the research question.","Choose two or three anchor findings.","For each finding, link the claim to exact evidence.","Explain plausible mechanisms or interpretations.","Consider alternative explanations.","Compare with relevant literature.","Explain at least two specific limitations and exactly how they restrict interpretation.","State realistic implications and specific future research."],"strong":"A strong Discussion makes reasoning visible and distinguishes what the study observed from what the author thinks may explain it.","examples":[{"label":"Correlational boundary","text":"The positive association is consistent with the proposed explanation, but unmeasured study habits and reverse direction remain plausible, so the result does not establish causation."},{"label":"Experimental boundary","text":"The treatment difference occurred under these laboratory conditions; limited replication and one organism type restrict broader generalization."}],"stuck":["Use the pattern Claim \u2192 Evidence \u2192 Reasoning \u2192 Alternative explanation \u2192 Boundary.","For each limitation finish 'This means I cannot confidently claim\u2026'","Compare studies on design and measurement, not only whether the direction matched."],"terms":["causal claim","confounder","generalization","external validity","attrition"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The same observational study shows a positive sleep-score association.","thinking":["My direct answer can say the variables were associated.","Possible explanations include sleep affecting learning, study routines affecting both, or reverse direction.","I need to explain how self-report and convenience sampling limit the claim."],"draft":"Higher reported sleep was associated with higher score in this sample; confounding, reverse direction, self-report error, and local sampling prevent a causal or broad population conclusion."}},"17":{"title":"Close the paper with a precise answer, then write the abstract from the finished study","plain":"The conclusion gives the controlled final answer. The abstract is a stand-alone summary of the whole paper and is usually easiest to write last.","steps":["Write a conclusion that answers the question at the level the design supports.","Include the central evidence without introducing a new analysis.","State an important boundary or limitation when needed.","Write the abstract: purpose/question, method/design, sample/system, central result, bounded conclusion.","Write a specific title that describes the actual study.","Choose keywords a reader might use to find the study."],"strong":"A strong abstract tells a reader what was studied, how, what was observed, and what can reasonably be concluded.","examples":[{"label":"Weak abstract ending","text":"The implications are discussed."},{"label":"Stronger function","text":"Add the central observed difference/association/theme in summarized form, then give the bounded conclusion."}],"stuck":["Imagine a reader sees only the abstract. Could they tell what you actually found?","Remove claims broader than your design or sample.","Make the title name the phenomenon/variables and useful context."],"terms":["generalization","causal claim","effect size","confidence interval"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The abstract currently has background and method but no finding.","thinking":["A reader should know what the study actually observed.","I can summarize the main estimate/pattern without reproducing calculations.","The conclusion must stay within the design."],"draft":"Add the primary result in one concise sentence, followed by a bounded conclusion that matches the observational/experimental design."}},"18":{"title":"Audit the paper as one evidence chain","plain":"Final proofreading is more than grammar. Every major claim should connect backward to evidence and every citation should support the nearby statement.","steps":["Trace the research question into the Method: did you actually collect evidence that answers it?","Trace Method into the dataset: do rows and columns represent the planned variables/units?","Trace dataset into analysis: does the analysis preserve pairing, repetition, clustering, and outcome type?","Trace analysis into Results: can every reported number be located?","Trace Results into Discussion and Conclusion: do claims stay within design and generalization limits?","Verify every in-text citation against the actual source and reference entry.","Run the final methodological and writing audit."],"strong":"A strong final paper forms a continuous, auditable chain from question to evidence to conclusion.","examples":[{"label":"Alignment problem","text":"Question asks whether X causes Y, but X was only observed. Fixing grammar cannot repair this; the causal wording must be revised."},{"label":"Citation problem","text":"A citation is present, but the cited study used a different population and does not support the universal claim. Narrow the claim or use appropriate evidence."}],"stuck":["Read only the research question and conclusion. Do they match?","Read only the Method and Results. Are all reported analyses based on planned/recorded data?","Open each source for any claim that feels stronger than the evidence."],"terms":["citation","causal claim","generalization","protocol","p-value","effect size"],"walkthrough":{"situation":"The conclusion says 'X caused Y,' but the Method describes an observational study.","thinking":["This is an alignment problem, not a grammar problem.","The research question, Results, Discussion, and title all need the same claim ceiling.","I should trace the wording backward through the design before final submission."],"draft":"Revise causal language to association language throughout the paper unless the design genuinely supports causal inference."}}},"fields":{"interest1":{"meaning":"One science-related topic, observation, problem, or claim that genuinely catches your attention.","write":"Write one concrete curiosity. It does not need to be a research question yet.","example":"I keep wondering why leaves on the same species look different in sunny and shaded areas.","questions":["Would I still care about this after spending several weeks on it?","Can I imagine any evidence that might help me learn about it?"],"avoid":"Do not write a topic just because it sounds academically impressive."},"problem1":{"meaning":"A specific situation within your interest that seems worth explaining, describing, comparing, or improving.","write":"Describe the observable problem or uncertainty in one or two sentences.","example":"Students often remember vocabulary for a quiz but forget it several days later.","questions":["What exactly is happening?","Who or what is affected?","What part is uncertain?"],"avoid":"Avoid writing only a broad social or scientific issue without a specific researchable problem."},"claim1":{"meaning":"A statement people make that could be examined with evidence.","write":"Write the claim as someone might say it, then keep an open mind about whether the evidence will support it.","example":"Using music while studying improves memory.","questions":["What evidence would make me more or less confident in this claim?","Does the claim imply causation?"],"avoid":"Do not treat the claim as true before investigating it."},"access1":{"meaning":"The evidence, materials, organisms, people, records, databases, or settings you could realistically access.","write":"List what you can access safely and with appropriate permission.","example":"School greenhouse plants, a classroom thermometer, peer-reviewed databases, and publicly available weather data.","questions":["Do I actually have permission to use this source of evidence?","Can I access it for the whole project timeline?"],"avoid":"Do not assume that being able to see data means you are authorized to use it for research."},"ideaA":{"meaning":"Your first possible research direction.","write":"Write a short direction that connects an interest to a possible kind of evidence.","example":"Compare water loss from identical soil containers placed under two light conditions.","questions":["What would I observe or measure?","Is this safe and feasible?"],"avoid":"Do not spend time making this wording perfect yet."},"ideaB":{"meaning":"A second possible research direction that is meaningfully different from Idea A.","write":"Generate another route, preferably using a different question, context, or type of evidence.","example":"Review published studies comparing retrieval practice and rereading in science learning.","questions":["Does this give me a real alternative?","Would this route fit my time/resources better?"],"avoid":"Avoid rewriting Idea A with only one word changed."},"ideaC":{"meaning":"A third possible research direction used to prevent premature commitment.","write":"Write another feasible option before you decide which project to pursue.","example":"Observe whether shaded and unshaded school surfaces differ in afternoon temperature.","questions":["What new evidence source does this idea use?","Would I enjoy investigating this more than A or B?"],"avoid":"Do not skip this simply because your first idea seems good."},"broadTopic":{"meaning":"The large subject area you are interested in. It should still be broader than the final project.","write":"Name a field-sized area, usually a short phrase.","example":"Plant stress; memory and learning; freshwater pollution.","questions":["What general area do I keep returning to?","Could many different research questions fit under this phrase?"],"avoid":"Do not try to make this the final research question."},"subtopic":{"meaning":"A narrower part of the broad topic that can become one project.","write":"Name the specific process, relationship, population, mechanism, or context you want to focus on.","example":"Broad topic: plant stress. Subtopic: early seedling responses to salinity.","questions":["What part of the broad topic can one student study well?","What am I deliberately leaving out?"],"avoid":"Avoid using the same wording as the broad topic."},"phenomenon":{"meaning":"The specific pattern, relationship, experience, process, or problem you want to understand.","write":"Describe what seems to happen, vary, or need explanation.","example":"Seedlings exposed to different salt concentrations appear to grow differently.","questions":["What exactly changes or varies?","What is the observable pattern?"],"avoid":"Avoid writing only a subject name such as \u201cplants.\u201d"},"contextPop":{"meaning":"Who, what, or where the possible study concerns.","write":"Name the population, organism, system, setting, and useful boundaries.","example":"Bean seedlings grown in classroom trays during a 7-day period.","questions":["Who or what would supply the evidence?","Where and when does the question apply?"],"avoid":"Do not use a population broader than you can justify."},"scope":{"meaning":"The part of the larger problem that this one project can reasonably address.","write":"State what you will focus on and what you will leave outside the project.","example":"Focus on early seedling growth over 7 days, not long-term plant fitness.","questions":["What can I complete well in the available time?","What tempting extra question should I deliberately leave out?"],"avoid":"Do not promise to solve the entire field."},"timeLimit":{"meaning":"The actual amount of time available for planning, collecting/retrieving evidence, analyzing, and writing.","write":"State the calendar or number of class periods/weeks you truly have.","example":"Six weeks total, with about eight class periods available for data collection.","questions":["Does my proposed measurement need longer than this?","Do I need time for pilot testing or repeated observations?"],"avoid":"Do not plan a long-term process that cannot occur during the course timeline."},"resources":{"meaning":"The equipment, software, databases, materials, expertise, or permissions the project needs.","write":"List what is required and note anything you do not yet have.","example":"Digital balance, nine containers, salinity solutions, spreadsheet software, teacher approval for laboratory space.","questions":["What resource would stop the project if unavailable?","Is there a lower-cost or safer alternative?"],"avoid":"Do not list resources you only hope will become available later as if they are guaranteed."},"safety":{"meaning":"Any physical, biological, chemical, privacy, participant, environmental, or authority issue that could make the project inappropriate without review.","write":"Name possible risks early, even if you think they can be controlled.","example":"Human caffeine assignment would create health/authority concerns; use a safer observational or literature-based route instead.","questions":["Could anyone be harmed, pressured, identified, or exposed?","Does the method involve organisms, chemicals, or procedures needing supervision?"],"avoid":"Do not wait until the final method to think about safety."},"topicChoice":{"meaning":"The research direction you are currently choosing to develop.","write":"State the chosen topic as a specific phrase and briefly keep its boundaries in mind.","example":"Early seedling growth under different salinity conditions.","questions":["Why is this more feasible or meaningful than the other ideas?","Can I already imagine a question and evidence source?"],"avoid":"Do not confuse the chosen topic with the final research question."},"concepts":{"meaning":"The main ideas in your possible research question that will become search concepts.","write":"List the two to four central concepts separately.","example":"retrieval practice; biology learning; delayed recall","questions":["Which words in my possible question carry the main meaning?","Which concept could have several research synonyms?"],"avoid":"Do not copy every word from the question, including unimportant connecting words."},"synonyms":{"meaning":"Alternative terms researchers may use for the same concepts.","write":"Build a small synonym bank for each concept.","example":"retrieval practice: practice testing, self-testing; delayed recall: retention, delayed test","questions":["What term would a journal article use?","Are there older/newer names for this idea?"],"avoid":"Do not join words that are related but mean different constructs as if they were true synonyms."},"prelimSearch":{"meaning":"A record of the early searches used to learn the field before the formal literature search.","write":"Write the search phrases and where you tried them, plus what each search taught you.","example":"Google Scholar: \"retrieval practice biology retention\" \u2192 learned that many papers use the term practice testing.","questions":["Did the search reveal better vocabulary?","Did it change the question?"],"avoid":"Do not treat this preliminary scan as proof that a literature gap exists."},"known":{"meaning":"What the preliminary literature repeatedly supports with reasonably consistent evidence.","write":"Summarize a pattern that appears across credible sources and keep the claim within their scope.","example":"Several studies report that retrieval practice can improve delayed recall under specific learning conditions.","questions":["How many sources support this?","Are their populations and measures comparable?"],"avoid":"Do not write \u201cresearch proves\u201d or make a universal claim from one source."},"methodsSeen":{"meaning":"Research methods or measurement approaches that appear in the preliminary literature.","write":"Note recurring designs, instruments, outcomes, timeframes, or analytic approaches.","example":"Many studies use pre/post or delayed tests; several compare recall scores after assigned study conditions.","questions":["How do researchers actually measure my construct?","Are there methods I cannot realistically reproduce?"],"avoid":"Do not copy a method simply because it is common without checking whether it fits your question."},"uncertain":{"meaning":"What remains unclear after the preliminary scan: inconsistent findings, missing contexts, measurement differences, or unresolved mechanisms.","write":"Describe the uncertainty precisely and identify what creates it.","example":"Studies use different retention intervals, so it is unclear whether the size of the retrieval-practice advantage is similar at 24 versus 48 hours.","questions":["What exactly is uncertain?","Could different methods explain the disagreement?"],"avoid":"Do not call every limitation a research gap."},"questionShift":{"meaning":"How your question changed after learning field vocabulary, methods, or limitations.","write":"Write the earlier idea and the improved direction, then explain what caused the change.","example":"Before: Does sleep improve grades? After: What is the association between self-reported weekday sleep duration and biology quiz score? The wording changed because sleep will be observed, not assigned.","questions":["What did I learn that made the old wording inaccurate?","Did I narrow the population, outcome, or claim?"],"avoid":"Do not pretend your question never changed; revision is part of research."},"rq1":{"meaning":"Your first complete research-question candidate.","write":"Write one answerable question using the best current wording.","example":"What is the association between weekly study time and delayed biology recall in this class?","questions":["What evidence would answer this?","Does it imply more than the design can support?"],"avoid":"Do not worry if this is not the final version."},"rq2":{"meaning":"A second wording or conceptual version of the research question.","write":"Change something meaningful such as scope, claim type, outcome, or population.","example":"How does delayed recall differ between two assigned study procedures under the same content conditions?","questions":["Is this question more feasible?","Does it require a different design?"],"avoid":"Do not create a second version by changing only punctuation."},"rq3":{"meaning":"A third candidate used to compare clarity and feasibility.","write":"Write another defensible question and compare it with the first two.","example":"How do students describe the study strategies they use after receiving assessment feedback?","questions":["Which question best matches my real curiosity?","Which can I answer ethically with available evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not select a question solely because it sounds complex."},"questionType":{"meaning":"The main kind of answer your research question asks for.","write":"Choose the family that best matches the question: description, association, causal effect, experience/process, or literature synthesis.","example":"\u201cIs sleep duration associated with score?\u201d \u2192 relationship/association.","questions":["Am I asking what exists, what relates, what causes, what people experience, or what the literature collectively says?"],"avoid":"Do not choose causal simply because you hope to show an effect."},"finalRQ":{"meaning":"The exact question your project is designed to answer with evidence.","write":"Write one clear sentence that names the key phenomenon/variables and useful population/context. Use wording compatible with the design.","example":"What is the association between reported weekday sleep duration and biology quiz score among students in this class?","questions":["Could the planned evidence answer every part of this sentence?","Does the wording imply causation that my design cannot support?"],"avoid":"Avoid vague questions such as \u201cHow does sleep affect students?\u201d"},"rqJustification":{"meaning":"Why the question can be answered responsibly within this project.","write":"Explain feasibility, access to evidence, ethics/safety, and why the question is scientifically or locally useful.","example":"The variables can be measured with a short anonymous survey and existing quiz scores only if teacher/privacy approval permits; the question is limited to association.","questions":["Do I have access to the needed evidence?","Is the project safe and permitted?","Can I finish it on time?"],"avoid":"Do not use \u201cinteresting\u201d as the only justification."},"searchBlock1":{"meaning":"One major concept from the research question plus alternative words researchers may use.","write":"List the main term and several true synonyms or closely related research terms.","example":"retrieval practice, practice testing, self-testing","questions":["What else might authors call this idea?","Are there spelling or terminology differences?"],"avoid":"Do not mix unrelated concepts in the same OR block."},"searchBlock2":{"meaning":"A second concept block for the formal literature search.","write":"List synonyms/closely equivalent terms for the second major idea.","example":"biology OR science OR STEM","questions":["Is this a different concept from Block 1?","Would removing this block make the search much broader?"],"avoid":"Do not connect synonyms with AND when OR is needed."},"searchBlock3":{"meaning":"An optional third concept block used only when it improves search precision without hiding relevant literature.","write":"Add a third concept and synonyms if the question genuinely needs it.","example":"retention OR recall OR delayed test","questions":["Does this block represent a necessary concept?","Am I over-restricting the search?"],"avoid":"Do not force a third block into every search."},"searchStrings":{"meaning":"The exact database queries you actually plan to use.","write":"Record the full Boolean strings, including parentheses/quotes when used.","example":"(\"retrieval practice\" OR \"practice testing\") AND (biology OR science) AND recall","questions":["Could another person repeat this search?","Did I use OR for synonyms and AND for different concepts?"],"avoid":"Do not write only a list of keywords if the actual search is more complex."},"databases":{"meaning":"The scholarly databases or search systems you will use.","write":"Name each system and, when helpful, explain why it is appropriate for the topic.","example":"ERIC and Education Source for education research; Scopus for broader interdisciplinary coverage.","questions":["Could one database miss an important part of the field?","Does the database index the type of studies I need?"],"avoid":"Do not rely on one general web search when the task requires a documented scholarly search."},"includeCriteria":{"meaning":"Rules a source must meet to enter your evidence set.","write":"Write criteria that can be applied consistently before reading whether the result supports your expectation.","example":"Peer-reviewed empirical studies; secondary/high-school or university learners; includes a delayed retention outcome.","questions":["Would I include a study with an unexpected result if it meets this rule?","Is each rule relevant to the question?"],"avoid":"Do not include papers simply because their result agrees with you."},"excludeCriteria":{"meaning":"Rules for removing sources that are outside the question or unusable for the planned review.","write":"State objective reasons for exclusion.","example":"Exclude editorials without original data and studies that do not report a learning outcome.","questions":["Could two people apply this rule similarly?","Is this about relevance/design, not whether I like the findings?"],"avoid":"Do not exclude inconvenient results."},"searchLog":{"meaning":"A reproducible record of each search attempt and how it changed.","write":"Record date, database, exact search string, filters, result count, screened/kept count, and what you changed next.","example":"2026-09-07 | ERIC | (retrieval practice OR practice testing) AND biology | peer reviewed | 84 results | 22 screened | 7 kept | add retention terms next.","questions":["Could someone repeat this search?","Can I explain why I revised the query?"],"avoid":"Do not record only the final successful query."},"theme1":{"meaning":"A cross-study pattern or idea supported by more than one source when possible.","write":"State the pattern, then identify which sources support, complicate, or contradict it.","example":"Retrieval-based practice is generally associated with stronger delayed retention across several tasks.","questions":["Which sources support this?","Which source does not fit cleanly?"],"avoid":"Do not make a theme that is simply one article title."},"theme2":{"meaning":"A second cross-study pattern that helps organize the literature.","write":"State a pattern distinct from Theme 1 and link it to source evidence.","example":"Feedback timing appears to change the size of retrieval-practice benefits.","questions":["Which studies contribute?","Does this overlap too much with Theme 1?"],"avoid":"Do not invent a theme before checking whether multiple studies support it."},"theme3":{"meaning":"An optional third cross-study pattern or methodological theme.","write":"Use it when the evidence base genuinely contains another useful pattern.","example":"Measurement method differs substantially across studies, limiting direct comparison of effect magnitude.","questions":["Is this a finding theme or a methods theme?","Does it help explain disagreement?"],"avoid":"Do not add themes merely to reach a target number."},"tensions":{"meaning":"Important differences, contradictions, or conditions that make the literature less simple.","write":"Describe what differs and what methodological/context feature may explain it.","example":"Benefits are smaller in studies using immediate tests; retention interval may be a boundary condition.","questions":["Do the studies use the same measure?","Do populations or designs differ?"],"avoid":"Do not treat disagreement as proof that one paper is wrong."},"repeatedLimits":{"meaning":"Limitations that recur across several studies and therefore shape what the literature can establish.","write":"Name repeated limitations and identify the studies in which they occur.","example":"Several studies use short retention intervals and convenience university samples.","questions":["Does this limitation actually recur?","How does it restrict the collective evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not copy each paper's limitation list without identifying patterns."},"gapType":{"meaning":"The most accurate kind of justification for doing your study.","write":"Choose the smallest defensible rationale. A project can be valuable without proving that no research exists.","example":"If prior work exists but uses university samples, choose extension to a new context/population rather than documented literature gap.","questions":["Am I repeating, extending, comparing methods, addressing inconsistency, or asking a local practical question?"],"avoid":"Do not choose 'documented literature gap' simply because it sounds strongest."},"gapStatement":{"meaning":"A precise statement of what your project will add, based on the evidence you actually reviewed.","write":"State a bounded gap, replication, extension, inconsistency, method comparison, or local question.","example":"Few included studies used a secondary biology classroom context; this project is a local extension of the established retrieval-practice question.","questions":["What source evidence supports this statement?","Would 'extension' be more accurate than 'no research exists'?"],"avoid":"Avoid global absence claims unless the search genuinely supports them."},"litToRQ":{"meaning":"The logical bridge from the literature synthesis to your exact research question.","write":"Explain how the patterns, limits, or rationale lead to the question you will ask.","example":"Because prior studies suggest a retrieval benefit but rarely examine this secondary biology context, the project asks whether the pattern appears in this classroom setting.","questions":["What literature evidence makes this question reasonable?","Is the question still broader than the reviewed evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not introduce a research question unrelated to the literature you just reviewed."},"litIntroPlan":{"meaning":"The job of the opening part of the literature review.","write":"Plan how you will define the topic, establish scope, and preview how the review is organized.","example":"Define retrieval practice and delayed retention, limit the review to science-learning contexts, then preview themes of retention, feedback, and measurement differences.","questions":["What does the reader need to understand before the themes?"],"avoid":"Do not begin with a list of article summaries."},"litThemePlan":{"meaning":"The order and purpose of the main synthesis sections or paragraphs.","write":"List each theme and what comparison it will make across sources.","example":"Theme 1: delayed retention pattern. Theme 2: feedback timing. Theme 3: measurement differences and comparability.","questions":["Does each theme answer part of the literature question?","Do the sections build logically?"],"avoid":"Do not organize only by author unless chronology itself is the research purpose."},"litTensionPlan":{"meaning":"Where and how the review will address disagreement, mixed evidence, or methodological tension.","write":"Plan the exact contradiction/qualification and which design differences may explain it.","example":"Two studies report smaller effects with immediate feedback; compare retention interval and scoring methods before calling the findings contradictory.","questions":["Which studies do not fit the main pattern?","Could measurement or population differences explain it?"],"avoid":"Do not hide evidence that complicates your preferred story."},"litEndPlan":{"meaning":"How the literature review will close and lead into the present study.","write":"Plan a short synthesis of what is established, what remains bounded/uncertain, and why the current question follows.","example":"Close by stating the consistent retention pattern, limits of context, and rationale for a local secondary biology extension.","questions":["Does the ending naturally lead to the research question?"],"avoid":"Do not add a new major theme in the final paragraph."},"litDraft":{"meaning":"Your working literature-review prose.","write":"Write paragraphs around claims about the literature, using traceable citations and comparison across sources.","example":"Across several studies, retrieval-based practice improved delayed recall, although effect magnitude varied with feedback and retention interval (citations).","questions":["What claim is this paragraph making about multiple studies?","Where is the qualification or comparison?"],"avoid":"Avoid one-source-per-paragraph summary chains when synthesis is needed."},"designType":{"meaning":"The overall structure used to obtain evidence.","write":"Choose the design family that matches the research question and how the key variables will be generated.","example":"Measured sleep and grades without assigning sleep \u2192 correlational/observational.","questions":["Is the key exposure assigned or only measured?","Am I studying numerical patterns, experiences, or existing literature?"],"avoid":"Do not label a study experimental just because it has groups."},"designWhy":{"meaning":"Your reasoning for why this design can answer the question.","write":"Connect the design directly to the wording of the research question.","example":"Because sleep is measured rather than assigned, an observational correlational design can estimate association but cannot establish a causal sleep effect.","questions":["What evidence will this design generate?","What claim will it not support?"],"avoid":"Do not write only \u201cbecause it is easiest.\u201d"},"comparisonStructure":{"meaning":"Exactly which groups, conditions, times, or variables are compared, or what is simply described.","write":"Name the structure clearly.","example":"Same students before and after correction; or different plants assigned to 0%, 1%, and 2% salt.","questions":["Same units or different units?","How many conditions/time points?"],"avoid":"Do not leave pairing/repetition implicit."},"claimBoundary":{"meaning":"The strongest conclusion the design could support and an important conclusion it cannot support.","write":"Write both the allowed and disallowed claim.","example":"Can estimate association in this sample; cannot establish that phone use causes lower scores.","questions":["What would a statistically significant result still not prove?","How far can the sample represent other populations?"],"avoid":"Do not let the p-value determine the causal claim."},"hypothesisNeeded":{"meaning":"Whether a formal prediction is appropriate for this study.","write":"Choose yes only when the study is genuinely testing a prespecified prediction that fits the design.","example":"Descriptive survey of species frequencies \u2192 a formal directional hypothesis may not be needed.","questions":["Is the project confirmatory and quantitative?","Would a hypothesis add real structure or just decoration?"],"avoid":"Do not invent a hypothesis because you think every paper must have one."},"researchHyp":{"meaning":"The scientific prediction you plan to evaluate, written before seeing the result.","write":"State the expected direction/pattern and variables, grounded in prior reasoning.","example":"Seedlings assigned to higher salt concentrations will show lower mean growth over 7 days.","questions":["What exactly do I expect to differ or relate?","What prior evidence or mechanism supports this?"],"avoid":"Do not write the observed result after the fact as if it was predicted."},"nullHyp":{"meaning":"The null model/hypothesis associated with a planned inferential test.","write":"State the statistical no-difference/no-association condition appropriate to the analysis.","example":"The mean paired change is 0; or the population correlation is 0.","questions":["What parameter does my test evaluate?","Does the test actually use this null?"],"avoid":"Do not treat the null as \u201cnothing is happening anywhere.\u201d"},"hypReason":{"meaning":"Your explanation for why a formal hypothesis is or is not appropriate.","write":"Connect the decision to the research question and design.","example":"A directional hypothesis is appropriate because the experimental comparison and prior evidence support a prespecified prediction about mean growth.","questions":["Is this confirmatory or exploratory?","Would a hypothesis change what I plan to test?"],"avoid":"Do not add a hypothesis as a decorative requirement."},"predictorIV":{"meaning":"The variable playing the explanatory role. In experiments it may be a manipulated independent variable; in observational studies it is usually a measured predictor/exposure.","write":"Name the variable and state whether it is assigned/manipulated or merely observed.","example":"Experimental: assigned salinity concentration. Observational: self-reported sleep duration.","questions":["Did I deliberately assign this variable?","If not, should I call it predictor/exposure?"],"avoid":"Do not use IV terminology automatically in observational work."},"outcomeDV":{"meaning":"The response measured to answer the question.","write":"Name the exact outcome, including units or coding when known.","example":"Change in seedling height in centimeters from Day 0 to Day 7.","questions":["What number/category/text will be recorded?","Does it directly answer the question?"],"avoid":"Avoid abstract outcomes with no measurement rule."},"operationalDefs":{"meaning":"The exact rules connecting abstract ideas to observable data.","write":"Write how every central variable is manipulated, measured, scored, or coded, including units/categories.","example":"Germination = visible radicle emergence by Day 7, coded 1=yes and 0=no.","questions":["Could another person apply this rule the same way?","What happens at ambiguous boundaries?"],"avoid":"Do not define a construct using another vague word."},"controlCondition":{"meaning":"The reference group/condition used for comparison, if the design needs one.","write":"Name the comparison and why it is the reference.","example":"0% salt solution as the reference condition.","questions":["What does the other condition get compared with?","Is a formal control actually necessary for this design?"],"avoid":"Do not confuse a control group with keeping procedures constant."},"controlledConditions":{"meaning":"Procedures or environmental features kept similar across conditions.","write":"List features that could influence the outcome and can reasonably be standardized.","example":"Same seed species/batch, solution volume, light period, temperature range, measurement schedule.","questions":["What else could change the outcome?","Which of those factors can I standardize?"],"avoid":"Do not claim a human characteristic is 'controlled' when it is merely measured or varies naturally."},"confounders":{"meaning":"Alternative factors that could explain an observed relationship or create group differences.","write":"Name plausible factors and how the design will prevent, balance, measure, or acknowledge them.","example":"Prior achievement may relate to both study time and exam score; measure it if appropriate or acknowledge it as a limitation.","questions":["What could cause both X and Y?","What differs between groups besides the intended factor?"],"avoid":"Do not list random irrelevant variables."},"experimentalUnit":{"meaning":"The smallest unit providing independent treatment/observation information for the key comparison.","write":"Name the object/person/group that could independently receive a different condition or exposure.","example":"If salt is assigned to dishes, dish is the experimental unit; seeds within the same dish may be subsamples.","questions":["What could have been assigned differently?","Are several measurements coming from the same underlying unit?"],"avoid":"Do not automatically use the number of rows as n."},"population":{"meaning":"The broader group/system your research question refers to.","write":"State the population at the scope you actually intend to discuss.","example":"All bean seedlings grown under the defined laboratory system; or students in this course context.","questions":["Who/what do I want the conclusion to concern?"],"avoid":"Do not write 'everyone' when you are studying one accessible class."},"sample":{"meaning":"The units that will actually be studied.","write":"State the actual number/type/source when known.","example":"30 students who voluntarily participate from one biology class; or 18 plants across 9 dishes.","questions":["Who or what is actually in the dataset?","How does this differ from the target population?"],"avoid":"Do not copy the population description if the actual sample is narrower."},"samplingMethod":{"meaning":"How the sample is selected or recruited.","write":"Name the method and explain the practical mechanism.","example":"Convenience sample from one available class; simple random sample from a complete roster if a genuine random draw is used.","questions":["Was every unit given a known selection chance?","Was the sample chosen because it was accessible?"],"avoid":"Do not say random when you mean convenient."},"sampleLimits":{"meaning":"How the sampling method limits what populations the results can represent.","write":"State the generalization boundary.","example":"Because the sample comes from one available class, findings should not be generalized to all secondary students.","questions":["What important groups/settings are not represented?","Could selection into the sample matter?"],"avoid":"Avoid claiming broad representativeness without a design that supports it."},"instrument":{"meaning":"The tool or procedure used to obtain a measurement.","write":"Name the actual sensor, rubric, survey, test, observation protocol, interview guide, or data source.","example":"Digital balance measuring mass to 0.01 g; prespecified scoring rubric for recall responses.","questions":["What physically or procedurally produces the recorded value?"],"avoid":"Do not write only the construct name."},"measureQuality":{"meaning":"Why the measurement is consistent enough and supports the intended interpretation.","write":"Discuss relevant reliability, validity, calibration, scoring consistency, or standardization.","example":"Use the same calibrated balance, zero before each measurement, and standardized weighing procedure.","questions":["Could two measurements differ because of the instrument/procedure?","Does the score truly represent the construct?"],"avoid":"Do not write 'valid because it seems accurate.'"},"ethicsIssues":{"meaning":"Any risk, privacy, authority, consent, coercion, environmental, or safety concern created by the method.","write":"List concerns even if you think they can be managed.","example":"Named student grades create identifiable educational records; participant recruitment by a teacher may create perceived pressure.","questions":["Could participation affect grades/status?","Could someone be identified?","Is any harmful exposure assigned?"],"avoid":"Do not assume access means permission."},"ethicsPlan":{"meaning":"How the identified ethics/safety issues will be handled and who must review/approve them.","write":"State specific safeguards and approval route.","example":"Use coded records, minimize identifiers, voluntary participation, non-grading consent management, and obtain school/instructor approval before collection.","questions":["Who has authority?","What data are truly necessary?","How can risk be reduced?"],"avoid":"Do not describe teacher review as formal institutional approval unless it actually is."},"materials":{"meaning":"The materials, instruments, software, datasets, forms, or resources needed to carry out the protocol.","write":"List enough detail to reproduce the work, including relevant amounts/specifications where they affect measurement.","example":"Nine labeled dishes, 18 seeds from one batch, prepared salinity solutions, ruler marked in millimeters, spreadsheet template.","questions":["Could another researcher reproduce the setup from this list?","Which specifications could affect the result?"],"avoid":"Do not include irrelevant brand details unless they matter scientifically."},"procedure":{"meaning":"The ordered actions used to collect or generate the evidence.","write":"Write the procedure in sequence, including assignment, timing, measurement, recording, and deviation handling.","example":"Randomly assign dishes to salinity levels; add equal solution volume; place dishes using randomized shelf positions; measure height at the same time daily; record by plant ID.","questions":["Where does assignment happen?","When exactly is each outcome measured?","What is recorded if something goes wrong?"],"avoid":"Do not write a vague recipe that leaves important research decisions implicit."},"replication":{"meaning":"How many independent units exist and how many repeated/technical observations are taken within them.","write":"Separate independent replication from repeats/subsamples.","example":"9 dishes are independent treatment units; 2 seeds measured per dish are subsamples, not n=18 independent treatment replicates.","questions":["How many units could independently receive different treatments?","How many measurements come from each unit?"],"avoid":"Do not multiply n by the number of repeated readings."},"methodControls":{"meaning":"Design protections used to reduce alternative explanations or measurement bias.","write":"Name only protections that actually apply and explain how they are implemented.","example":"Randomly assign dishes to salinity conditions; randomize position on shelf; standardize solution volume; blind scorer to condition if feasible.","questions":["What bias/threat does each protection address?"],"avoid":"Do not list 'randomization' unless a random mechanism will be used."},"rowUnit":{"meaning":"The exact meaning of one row in the dataset.","write":"Write one sentence: 'One row represents\u2026'","example":"One row represents one plant at one measurement time; plant_id links repeated rows.","questions":["Can the same independent unit appear in several rows?","What ID is needed to preserve that relationship?"],"avoid":"Do not assume one row equals one independent unit."},"columns":{"meaning":"The variables/fields that will be stored in the dataset.","write":"List each column with type and units/categories.","example":"plant_id (ID), dish_id (ID), salinity_pct (numeric), day (count), height_cm (numeric), germinated (binary 0/1).","questions":["Does every research variable appear?","Are IDs included for repeated/nested data?"],"avoid":"Avoid ambiguous column names such as data1 or result."},"missingRule":{"meaning":"The rule for representing and handling values that are genuinely missing.","write":"State what counts as missing, how it is coded, and whether complete-case or another approach will be used later.","example":"Blank means not observed; 0 is a real value and will never be used to mean missing.","questions":["Could 0 be a real value?","Why might data be missing?"],"avoid":"Do not invent replacement values just to fill blanks."},"exclusionRule":{"meaning":"A rule decided before results are known for excluding units/observations from a particular analysis.","write":"State objective criteria and preserve a log of exclusions.","example":"Exclude measurements only when the sensor recorded an explicit error code; retain biologically extreme but valid values.","questions":["Would I apply the same rule if the result helped my hypothesis?"],"avoid":"Do not remove values because they make p larger."},"analysisIntent":{"meaning":"The planned way you expect to summarize or analyze the data before final results are known.","write":"State the primary estimand and likely analysis family at an appropriate level of detail.","example":"Primary quantity: mean 7-day growth difference across assigned salinity conditions; describe group distributions and use an analysis that respects dish-level treatment assignment.","questions":["What exact quantity answers the question?","Does the planned analysis preserve unit structure?"],"avoid":"Do not choose a method because you expect it to produce a small p-value."},"rawLocation":{"meaning":"Where the original unchanged data file is kept.","write":"Give a specific storage location or naming convention that protects the raw record.","example":"School-approved Drive folder / raw_data_2026-09-04.csv, read-only copy retained.","questions":["Could I reconstruct what the original data looked like?"],"avoid":"Do not overwrite the only raw file with cleaned values."},"missingObserved":{"meaning":"Which data are missing and how the analysis handled them.","write":"Describe amount/pattern and actual handling.","example":"2 of 30 students lacked follow-up data; paired analysis used 28 complete pairs.","questions":["How many values/units are missing?","Could missingness affect interpretation?"],"avoid":"Do not say 'none' unless checked."},"errorsCorrections":{"meaning":"Any data error, correction, exclusion, or protocol deviation discovered after collection.","write":"Keep an audit trail of original value, corrected value, reason, and decision.","example":"Row 14 height entered as 55.0 cm; source sheet showed 5.5 cm. Corrected with original value logged.","questions":["Can I prove why the correction was made?"],"avoid":"Never silently rewrite inconvenient values."},"plots":{"meaning":"Visual displays used to understand distribution, relationships, or repeated structure before inference.","write":"Name the plot and what you inspected.","example":"Scatterplot of sleep hours vs score; paired line plot for pre/post; histogram of growth.","questions":["What pattern or problem could this plot reveal?"],"avoid":"Do not use a bar chart automatically for every numerical comparison."},"descriptives":{"meaning":"Summaries describing the observed data before or alongside inference.","write":"Report appropriate summaries for the variable type.","example":"Numerical: n, mean, SD, median, IQR, min/max. Binary: count and proportion in each category.","questions":["Is this variable numerical or categorical?","Is the distribution skewed?"],"avoid":"Do not calculate mean/SD for arbitrary category labels."},"primaryEstimand":{"meaning":"The exact quantity that, if estimated, would answer the research question.","write":"Write the quantity in ordinary language before naming a test.","example":"Mean difference in 7-day growth between 0% and 2% salt conditions; or correlation between sleep hours and quiz score.","questions":["What number would I most want to know even if I never calculated a p-value?"],"avoid":"Do not write only the name of a statistical test."},"analysisChoice":{"meaning":"The primary analysis and why it matches the design, units, and outcome type.","write":"Name the method and justify using design structure.","example":"Paired t-test for a numerical before/after outcome from the same students because the paired difference is the estimand.","questions":["Same units or different units?","What type is the outcome?","What quantity am I estimating?"],"avoid":"Do not pick a test by searching for the one that gives significance."},"assumptionChecks":{"meaning":"The design and data conditions you checked before interpreting the analysis.","write":"State the relevant assumptions and what plots/design information showed.","example":"For paired t-test, inspect distribution of difference scores and extreme paired differences; pairing is preserved by student ID.","questions":["Which assumption comes from design rather than a normality test?","What did the plot show?"],"avoid":"Do not write simply 'data are normal' without saying what was checked."},"effectSizePlan":{"meaning":"How you will report magnitude, not only statistical significance.","write":"Name the raw effect/association and a standardized effect only if useful.","example":"Report raw mean difference with 95% CI and Hedges' g for a two-group mean comparison.","questions":["What unit makes the result scientifically understandable?","Would a standardized effect add useful comparison?"],"avoid":"Do not treat p-value as an effect size."},"multiplicity":{"meaning":"How you will handle several statistical tests or exploratory comparisons.","write":"State which analysis is primary and how additional tests will be labeled or adjusted when needed.","example":"Primary comparison defined in advance; exploratory pairwise comparisons labeled exploratory and adjusted for multiplicity if used.","questions":["How many opportunities am I giving myself to find significance?","Which test directly answers the main question?"],"avoid":"Do not hide how many analyses were tried."},"analysisSample":{"meaning":"The exact observations/units included in the final analysis after documented exclusions/missingness.","write":"State n and any difference from the collected sample.","example":"Collected 30 students; primary paired analysis includes 28 complete pairs because 2 lacked follow-up.","questions":["Why is analyzed n different from enrolled/collected n?"],"avoid":"Do not hide exclusions."},"result1":{"meaning":"The main result that directly answers the research question, tied to exact output/table/figure.","write":"Report estimate/pattern and traceable source.","example":"Mean paired change = 2.1 points, 95% CI [...]; see Analysis A1 / Table 1.","questions":["What exact output supports this sentence?","Does this directly answer the primary question?"],"avoid":"Do not write an explanation of why it happened here."},"result2":{"meaning":"A secondary result that is relevant but not the main answer to the research question.","write":"Report it with the same neutral, traceable style as the primary result and label exploratory analyses when appropriate.","example":"Germination proportion was also lower at the highest salinity level; see Table 2.","questions":["Was this outcome prespecified?","What analysis/table supports it?"],"avoid":"Do not let secondary findings replace the primary result simply because they are more dramatic."},"unexpected":{"meaning":"A null, weak, contradictory, or surprising result that still needs transparent reporting.","write":"State it neutrally and exactly.","example":"No clear difference was observed in the secondary outcome; CI included values in both directions.","questions":["Was this analysis planned or exploratory?","Does uncertainty prevent a strong no-effect claim?"],"avoid":"Do not omit a result because it does not support the hypothesis."},"resultsDraft":{"meaning":"The working Results section of the paper.","write":"Organize results in the order of the research question/analysis plan, using exact estimates and references to tables/figures.","example":"Begin with analyzed sample and descriptives, then primary analysis, then clearly labeled secondary results.","questions":["Can every number be traced to stored output?","Have I kept interpretation out?"],"avoid":"Do not explain why a result happened in this section."},"directAnswer":{"meaning":"The first controlled answer to the research question in the Discussion.","write":"Answer using the strongest language the design supports.","example":"Higher reported sleep was associated with higher quiz score in this sample; the observational design does not establish a causal sleep effect.","questions":["Does this wording match the research question?","Does it stay within the claim boundary?"],"avoid":"Avoid 'proved' and universal claims."},"interpret1":{"meaning":"Your explanation of what the first anchor finding may mean scientifically or practically.","write":"Connect the exact result to a plausible interpretation while preserving uncertainty.","example":"The lower growth under higher salinity is consistent with osmotic/ionic stress under these conditions.","questions":["What mechanism or explanation fits the observed pattern?","What evidence in this study directly supports that interpretation?"],"avoid":"Do not present a plausible explanation as if the study directly measured the mechanism."},"interpret2":{"meaning":"Your interpretation of another important finding, especially one that adds complexity or qualification.","write":"Explain the second finding and how it changes the overall answer.","example":"The germination pattern was weaker than the growth pattern, suggesting the treatment may have affected post-germination growth more clearly than emergence in this short timeframe.","questions":["Does this finding support, complicate, or contradict the main result?"],"avoid":"Do not ignore a finding because it is less convenient."},"litCompare":{"meaning":"How your results compare with specific prior research.","write":"State where your findings are consistent, different, or difficult to compare, and use design/method details to explain why.","example":"The direction is similar to two prior studies, but direct magnitude comparison is limited because they used different salinity ranges and growth measures.","questions":["Are the outcomes measured the same way?","Are populations/conditions comparable?"],"avoid":"Do not write only 'this agrees with Smith' without explaining the basis of comparison."},"alternatives":{"meaning":"Other plausible explanations for the observed pattern besides your preferred interpretation.","write":"Name realistic alternatives connected to the design.","example":"Students who sleep more may also have different study routines or workloads; reverse direction is also possible.","questions":["What unmeasured factor could explain both variables?","Could the direction of influence be reversed?"],"avoid":"Do not list implausible possibilities merely to fill space."},"limitations":{"meaning":"Specific design/measurement/sampling limits and how each restricts the conclusion.","write":"For each limitation, state the mechanism and the exact claim it weakens.","example":"Self-reported sleep may contain measurement error, limiting precision of the estimated association; one-class convenience sample limits generalization.","questions":["How could this feature bias or limit the result?","What claim becomes weaker because of it?"],"avoid":"Avoid generic 'small sample is a limitation' without explaining why."},"implications":{"meaning":"Reasonable actions or interpretations supported by the evidence and its limits.","write":"State who/what the implication applies to and avoid claiming more than the study showed.","example":"The result supports further study of sleep and learning in this local context; it does not justify prescribing sleep changes based on this observational sample alone.","questions":["Who can use this finding?","What action would require stronger evidence?"],"avoid":"Do not turn statistical significance into a policy recommendation."},"future":{"meaning":"A specific next study that addresses a limitation or unanswered mechanism.","write":"Propose a design improvement directly connected to current evidence.","example":"Replicate with a larger multi-class sample and objective sleep measurement; examine prior achievement as a prespecified covariate.","questions":["Which current limitation can the next study address?","What new evidence would distinguish alternative explanations?"],"avoid":"Avoid vague 'more research is needed.'"},"discussionDraft":{"meaning":"The working Discussion section.","write":"Build from direct answer to anchor findings, reasoning, literature comparison, limitations, implications, and future research.","example":"Paragraph 1 answers the question; paragraphs 2\u20133 interpret anchor findings; later paragraphs address alternatives, literature, limitations, implications, and future work.","questions":["Is every interpretation connected to an actual result?","Are causal/generalization limits explicit?"],"avoid":"Do not repeat the Results section with extra adjectives."},"conclusionDraft":{"meaning":"The short final answer that closes the paper using evidence already presented.","write":"Restate the main answer at the correct claim level, include an important boundary, and avoid new results.","example":"Higher salinity was associated with lower growth under the studied laboratory conditions; the limited unit replication restricts broader generalization.","questions":["Does this answer the exact research question?","Have I introduced anything new?"],"avoid":"Do not make the conclusion broader or more certain than the Discussion."},"abstractDraft":{"meaning":"A stand-alone summary of the complete study, usually written after the paper is stable.","write":"Include purpose/question, design/method, sample/system, central result, and bounded conclusion.","example":"Purpose\u2026 Method\u2026 Main result with summarized estimate/pattern\u2026 Conclusion within design limits.","questions":["Could someone tell what was actually found?","Is there any calculation detail that belongs in Results instead?"],"avoid":"Do not write an abstract with only background and no result."},"titleDraft":{"meaning":"A specific, accurate title that helps a reader know what was studied.","write":"Name the central variables/phenomenon and useful context/design information.","example":"Reported Sleep Duration and Biology Quiz Scores in a Secondary Classroom: An Observational Study","questions":["Does the title imply causation the design cannot support?","Can I remove vague words such as 'A Study of'?"],"avoid":"Avoid sensational or causal titles for observational evidence."},"keywords":{"meaning":"A small set of searchable terms that describe the central topic, variables, population/context, or method.","write":"Choose commonly used research terms, usually several concise phrases.","example":"retrieval practice; biology education; delayed recall; secondary students","questions":["Would a researcher search this phrase to find my paper?","Are these terms more informative than words already obvious from 'research' or 'study'?"],"avoid":"Do not use full sentences or extremely general terms such as science, data, and research."},"citationStyle":{"meaning":"The formatting system required by your teacher, journal, or discipline.","write":"Select the required style and follow its official rules consistently.","example":"APA 7 if your course instructions require APA seventh edition.","questions":["What does the assignment explicitly require?","Did the teacher provide a special local format?"],"avoid":"Do not choose a style based on personal preference when the assignment specifies one."},"citationAudit":{"meaning":"A list of citation/reference problems that still require checking against actual sources.","write":"Record missing matches, incomplete bibliographic data, unsupported claims, or formatting issues.","example":"(Garcia, 2022) appears in text but no verified source record exists; verify before submission.","questions":["Does every in-text citation have a reference entry?","Does the source support the exact nearby claim?"],"avoid":"Do not invent a DOI or missing author information."},"finalEdits":{"meaning":"The final methodological and writing changes needed before submission.","write":"List concrete actions, not vague intentions.","example":"Change causal wording in Discussion paragraph 2; verify S06 DOI; update Results n from 30 to 28 complete pairs.","questions":["What still prevents the paper from being auditable?"],"avoid":"Avoid writing only 'proofread.'"}},"labs":{"literature":{"search":{"title":"Search log \u2014 what you are doing","text":"Record exactly where and how you searched. A search log lets you distinguish 'I did not find it' from 'it probably does not exist.'","terms":["boolean search","literature gap"]},"sources":{"title":"Source record \u2014 what each paper contributes","text":"Capture enough information to compare studies later: design, population/context, variables/constructs, measures, findings, limitations, and source quality.","terms":["TRAPP","observational study","measurement validity"],"decisionKeys":["sourceType"]},"screen":{"title":"Screening \u2014 apply rules consistently","text":"Include or exclude sources using prespecified relevance/design criteria. Do not keep a paper because its result agrees with your expectation.","terms":["sampling method","literature gap"],"decisionKeys":["screeningStatus"]},"study":{"title":"Study matrix \u2014 compare like with like","text":"Read across rows to compare populations, methods, measures, and findings. Differences in methods can explain apparent disagreement.","terms":["synthesis","measurement validity"]},"synth":{"title":"Synthesis \u2014 build claims across studies","text":"A theme should connect multiple sources and also record conflicting or mixed evidence. The question is 'what does the evidence collectively support, and under what conditions?'","terms":["synthesis","theme","generalization"],"decisionKeys":["themeStance"]},"gap":{"title":"Gap/rationale \u2014 make the smallest claim the evidence supports","text":"A legitimate project can be replication, extension, local investigation, method comparison, or exploration of inconsistent findings. Global novelty is not required.","terms":["literature gap","replication","generalization"]},"claims":{"title":"Claim audit \u2014 every literature statement needs evidence","text":"Link a claim to the exact source records that support it, then add a boundary when the sources apply only to particular populations, measures, or designs.","terms":["citation","causal claim","generalization"]},"outline":{"title":"Paragraph planning \u2014 give each paragraph a reasoning job","text":"Plan a claim about the literature, supporting studies, comparison/qualification, and the implication for the next paragraph or your study.","terms":["synthesis","theme"]}},"methods":{"design":{"title":"Design \u2014 start with what you need to know","text":"Decide whether the key factor is assigned or observed, whether the same unit appears in several conditions/times, and what causal claim ceiling follows.","terms":["experimental unit","observational study","random assignment","causal claim","paired data"],"decisionKeys":["mdAssignment","mdSameUnit"]},"vars":{"title":"Variables and constructs \u2014 turn ideas into records","text":"A construct such as stress or growth needs a role and an operational definition. Design determines whether 'independent variable' is appropriate terminology.","terms":["construct","operational definition","independent variable","predictor","outcome"]},"rep":{"title":"Units and replication \u2014 identify what is truly independent","text":"Separate independently treated/observed units from repeated readings, technical replicates, and subsamples. This prevents pseudoreplication.","terms":["experimental unit","replication","technical replicate","subsample","pseudoreplication","repeated measures"]},"sample":{"title":"Sampling \u2014 who/what enters the study","text":"Sampling affects whom the result can represent. Random sampling and random assignment are different processes with different purposes.","terms":["population","sample","sampling frame","random sampling","random assignment","generalization"],"decisionKeys":["msMethod"]},"measure":{"title":"Measurement \u2014 define exactly what produces the data","text":"For every construct, specify instrument/procedure, operational rule, unit/scale, timing, reliability, validity, and relevant calibration/scoring protections.","terms":["reliability","measurement validity","calibration","scale","binary","ordinal"]},"ethics":{"title":"Ethics and safety \u2014 permission comes before data","text":"Access to classmates, grades, video, health information, or interventions does not automatically mean a student researcher is authorized to use them. Identify risk, consent, privacy, and approval before collection.","terms":["identifiable data","generalization"],"decisionKeys":["meHuman","meMinors","meIdent","meSensitive","meIntervention"]},"procedure":{"title":"Procedure \u2014 remove hidden guesses","text":"Write ordered actions, what is recorded, and what counts as a deviation. Another researcher should not have to invent missing decisions.","terms":["protocol","blinding","counterbalancing","random assignment"]},"data":{"title":"Data table \u2014 define rows before columns","text":"First state what one row represents. Then create columns with IDs, data types, units/categories, coding rules, and missing-value rules.","terms":["data dictionary","identifier","binary","categorical","ordinal","numerical","missing data"]},"audit":{"title":"Audit and lock \u2014 check the evidence chain before collection","text":"Critical issues should be resolved before locking. Locking preserves the version of the plan that existed before final data were seen.","terms":["protocol","experimental unit","measurement validity","generalization"]}},"data":{"import":{"title":"Import \u2014 preserve the raw evidence","text":"Use a copy for analysis and keep the original file unchanged elsewhere. Stable IDs are essential for paired, repeated, or nested observations.","terms":["identifier","paired data","repeated measures","missing data"]},"quality":{"title":"Data quality \u2014 investigate before deleting","text":"Missing values, duplicates, unusual values, and coding problems are reasons to inspect the source and apply documented rules. They are not automatic deletion commands.","terms":["missing data","outlier","data dictionary"]},"explore":{"title":"Explore \u2014 see the data before reducing them to one number","text":"Numerical variables need distribution/relationship plots; categorical variables need counts/proportions. Paired data should preserve links between the same units.","terms":["numerical","categorical","binary","paired data","descriptive statistics"]},"setup":{"title":"Analysis setup \u2014 define the target quantity first","text":"Choose what you want to estimate, identify variable type and independence/paired structure, then choose the analysis family.","terms":["estimand","binary","ordinal","nominal","independent groups","paired data"],"decisionKeys":["analysisStructure","outcomeType"]},"run":{"title":"Run analysis \u2014 map the actual columns to the design","text":"The same test name can be wrong if the columns do not represent the planned units. Confirm what each selected column means before running.","terms":["predictor","outcome","paired data","p-value","effect size","confidence interval"],"decisionKeys":["correlationCoefficient","anovaModel","fisherChoice"]},"history":{"title":"Analysis record \u2014 keep decisions traceable","text":"Store the primary analysis with its variable mapping, assumptions, estimate, uncertainty, and warnings. Do not choose a stored run merely because it has the smallest p-value.","terms":["p-value","effect size","confidence interval","inference"]},"audit":{"title":"Results audit \u2014 question, unit, estimand, analysis, result","text":"A defensible result answers the same question the study was designed to answer and preserves the true unit structure.","terms":["estimand","experimental unit","generalization"]}},"writing":{"intro":{"title":"Introduction \u2014 lead the reader to the question","text":"Move from the problem and key constructs to synthesized prior evidence, a bounded rationale/gap, and the exact study purpose/question.","terms":["construct","synthesis","literature gap"]},"lit":{"title":"Literature review \u2014 write relationships among studies","text":"Organize around ideas or methodological comparisons. Source-by-source summaries should not dominate.","terms":["synthesis","theme","citation"]},"method":{"title":"Method \u2014 report what was actually planned/done","text":"Use the locked protocol as factual evidence. Do not quietly add a procedure to the paper that was not part of the study.","terms":["protocol","operational definition","experimental unit"]},"results":{"title":"Results \u2014 report evidence without explaining it away","text":"State the observed estimates/patterns and trace them to analysis records. Explanations and implications belong in Discussion.","terms":["effect size","confidence interval","p-value"]},"discussion":{"title":"Discussion \u2014 make reasoning and limits explicit","text":"Answer the question, interpret anchor findings, consider alternatives, compare literature, analyze limitations, and calibrate claims.","terms":["causal claim","confounder","generalization"]},"closing":{"title":"Conclusion and abstract \u2014 finish from the evidence","text":"The conclusion gives the controlled answer. The abstract summarizes purpose, method, central result, and bounded conclusion.","terms":["generalization","effect size"]},"citations":{"title":"Citation audit \u2014 presence is not enough","text":"A citation must correspond to a real source and the source must support the nearby claim at the stated scope.","terms":["citation","generalization"]},"audit":{"title":"Whole-paper audit \u2014 one continuous evidence chain","text":"Question \u2192 literature \u2192 method \u2192 data \u2192 analysis \u2192 results \u2192 discussion \u2192 conclusion should remain logically consistent.","terms":["protocol","estimand","causal claim"]}}},"choices":{"questionType":{"title":"How do I choose the question family?","prompt":"Choose based on the kind of answer you need, not which label sounds most scientific.","options":{"Descriptive":"Choose when you want to describe what exists, how common something is, or how values are distributed. Example: What proportion of seeds germinate at each salinity?","Correlational / observational":"Choose when key variables are measured as they naturally occur and you want to examine a relationship. Example: Is sleep duration associated with quiz score?","Experimental":"Choose when the researcher deliberately assigns/manipulates a condition and the design is built to estimate an effect. Manipulation alone is not enough; assignment and validity protections still matter.","Qualitative":"Choose when you want to understand experiences, meanings, processes, explanations, or perspectives using evidence such as interviews, open responses, or observations.","Literature review":"Choose when your evidence will come from synthesizing existing studies and you are not collecting new participant/experimental data.","Meta-analysis":"Choose only for a quantitative review in which sufficiently comparable effect estimates from multiple studies can be combined using an explicit statistical model. This is an advanced route.","Mixed methods / unsure":"Mixed methods means deliberately integrating quantitative and qualitative evidence to answer connected parts of the same problem. Choose unsure if you genuinely have not resolved the design yet."},"questions":["Am I collecting new data or synthesizing existing studies?","Will I assign/manipulate the key factor?","Am I asking about a relationship, an effect, a description, or an experience/process?"]},"gapType":{"title":"What kind of study rationale do I actually have?","prompt":"Use the smallest claim the literature can support.","options":{"Documented literature gap":"Use when a sufficiently broad, documented search shows an important question, population, measure, or relationship has not been adequately addressed. Do not use from a quick search.","Replication":"Use when you intentionally repeat a prior design/question to see whether a result can be reproduced under similar conditions.","Extension to a new context/population":"Use when prior work exists and you study the question in a meaningfully different population, setting, measure, or condition.","Method comparison":"Use when the project compares methods, instruments, scoring approaches, or procedures.","Resolve or explore inconsistent findings":"Use when credible studies disagree or vary and your study investigates a plausible reason or adds evidence to that uncertainty.","Local/practical question":"Use when the project addresses a real local decision or context even if the broad topic is already well studied.","Learning-focused investigation":"Use when the primary purpose is a carefully bounded educational investigation of research reasoning/methods.","Other":"Use when none fits, then explain the rationale precisely."},"questions":["Do I truly know something is absent from the literature?","Would replication or extension describe the project more accurately?","Is the value mainly local or practical?"]},"designType":{"title":"How do I choose the primary design?","prompt":"Start with what the researcher actually does to generate evidence.","options":{"Descriptive":"Measures or documents a phenomenon without a primary relationship/effect comparison.","Correlational / observational":"Measures predictors/exposures and outcomes without assigning the key exposure.","Experimental":"Researcher assigns/manipulates conditions with design protections aimed at estimating a causal effect.","Quasi-experimental":"There is an intervention or exposure comparison, but assignment is not fully randomized; causal interpretation needs stronger qualification.","Qualitative":"Uses systematic non-numerical evidence to understand meanings, experiences, processes, or explanations.","Mixed methods":"Intentionally integrates quantitative and qualitative evidence, with a reason for how the strands answer connected questions.","Literature review":"Synthesizes existing research without quantitative pooling of effect estimates.","Meta-analysis":"Quantitatively combines comparable estimates across studies using a meta-analytic model.","Undecided":"Use temporarily if important design information is still unresolved."},"questions":["Is the key factor assigned or observed?","Are the same units measured repeatedly?","What is the strongest claim I need the design to support?"]},"hypothesisNeeded":{"title":"Does my study need a formal hypothesis?","prompt":"A hypothesis is useful when a specific prediction is genuinely part of the study logic.","options":{"Yes":"Choose for a confirmatory study with a prespecified, evidence-based prediction that can be tested by the planned design.","No":"Choose for descriptive, many exploratory qualitative, and some review questions where forcing a prediction would add no scientific value.","Unsure":"Choose while you decide whether the question is confirmatory or exploratory. Explain the reason before locking the method."},"questions":["Am I testing a prediction or first describing/exploring a phenomenon?","Would I write this prediction before seeing the data?"]},"citationStyle":{"title":"Which citation style should I use?","prompt":"This is usually decided by the assignment, discipline, journal, or teacher.","options":{"APA 7":"Use when the assignment requires American Psychological Association seventh edition style.","MLA":"Use when explicitly required by the course/discipline.","Chicago":"Use when explicitly required by the course/discipline.","Other / teacher-specific":"Use when your teacher provides a different format or local instructions."},"questions":["What does the assignment sheet say?","Did the teacher provide a reference example?"]},"mdAssignment":{"title":"How do units reach conditions or exposures?","prompt":"This determines what kind of comparison you really have.","options":{"Random assignment":"A random process places experimental units into conditions.","Nonrandom assignment":"The researcher assigns conditions, but not through a random process.","Naturally occurring / observed exposure":"The researcher does not assign the key exposure; it is measured as it already exists.","Single-group / no assignment":"There is one group/condition or no between-condition assignment.","Not applicable":"Assignment does not make sense for this design, such as many qualitative or literature-review studies."},"questions":["Could the same unit have been assigned to another condition by the researcher?","Was a random mechanism actually used?"]},"mdSameUnit":{"title":"Do the same units appear more than once?","prompt":"This decides whether observations are paired/repeated.","options":{"yes":"Choose if the same person, plant, animal, sample, classroom, or other unit contributes observations across multiple times or conditions.","no":"Choose if each independent unit contributes to only one comparison condition/time structure.","not applicable":"Choose if repeated-condition logic does not apply to the design."},"questions":["Can I match observation A and B to the same underlying unit?","Does one ID appear in several conditions or times?"]},"msMethod":{"title":"How was the sample selected?","prompt":"Use the actual mechanism, not the method you wish you had.","options":{"Census / all available units":"You include every unit in the defined accessible population/frame.","Simple random / probability sample":"Every unit in a defined frame has a known probability of selection through a random process.","Stratified probability sample":"Units are divided into strata, then probability sampled within strata.","Cluster sample":"Groups/clusters are sampled and units within selected clusters are studied according to the plan.","Systematic sample":"Units are selected using a fixed interval from an ordered frame with an appropriate starting process.","Convenience sample":"Units enter because they are easy/available to access.","Purposive / criterion sample":"Units are deliberately selected because they meet criteria important to the research question, common in qualitative work.","Snowball / network sample":"Existing participants or contacts help identify additional participants.","Other":"Describe the exact selection mechanism."},"questions":["Was there a real probability mechanism?","Was access/convenience the main reason these units entered?"]},"meHuman":{"title":"Does this involve human participants or linkable human records?","prompt":"Answer yes if the project interacts with people for research data or uses records that can be linked to them.","options":{"yes":"Includes surveys, interviews, observations of identifiable people, student records, health/behavior data, or interventions involving people.","no":"No human participants and no identifiable/linkable human records are used."},"questions":["Could the data be connected to a person directly or indirectly?"]},"meMinors":{"title":"Are any participants under the applicable age of legal adulthood?","prompt":"Minors often require additional permission/assent safeguards.","options":{"yes":"At least one participant is a minor.","no":"All participants are adults under the applicable policy.","not applicable":"There are no human participants."},"questions":["What age/policy rules apply in the actual institution?"]},"meIdent":{"title":"Could the data identify or be linked back to a person?","prompt":"Identification can be direct or indirect.","options":{"yes":"Names, IDs, faces, voices, precise location, contact information, or linkable records are collected/retained.","no":"The planned data do not contain direct identifiers and cannot reasonably be linked back using retained information."},"questions":["Could someone combine fields to identify a participant?","Is a code key retained somewhere?"]},"meSensitive":{"title":"Does the project collect sensitive personal information?","prompt":"Consider whether disclosure could create harm, stigma, disciplinary consequences, or privacy concerns.","options":{"yes":"Includes sensitive health, behavior, identity, academic, family, financial, or similarly private information under the relevant policy/context.","no":"The information collected is not sensitive under the relevant policy/context."},"questions":["Would a participant reasonably expect this information to stay private?","Could disclosure cause harm?"]},"meIntervention":{"title":"Will the student researcher assign an intervention to people?","prompt":"This includes deliberately changing what participants receive or do for research purposes.","options":{"yes":"The research plan assigns/manipulates a treatment, behavior, exposure, instruction, substance, or other condition involving people.","no":"The project observes existing conditions/behaviors or uses non-interventional evidence."},"questions":["Am I asking participants to do something they would not otherwise do for the study?","Could the intervention create physical, psychological, academic, or social risk?"]},"sourceType":{"title":"What kind of source is this?","prompt":"Classify what the document actually is because different source types support different claims.","options":{"Peer-reviewed empirical article":"Reports original data collected/analyzed by the authors.","Systematic review / meta-analysis":"Uses explicit methods to identify and synthesize prior studies; meta-analysis also quantitatively pools estimates.","Scholarly review":"Synthesizes prior scholarship but may not use a systematic review protocol.","Government / institutional report":"Issued by a public/institutional body; evaluate methods and purpose rather than assuming quality.","Book / chapter":"Long-form scholarly source; usefulness depends on recency, authorship, evidence, and purpose.","Other":"Classify and evaluate the source explicitly."}},"screeningStatus":{"title":"What does screening status mean?","prompt":"The status records where a source stands under your inclusion/exclusion rules.","options":{"Included":"Meets the current eligibility criteria and remains in the evidence set.","Pending":"Not enough information has been checked yet to decide.","Excluded":"Does not meet a stated eligibility criterion; record the reason."}},"themeStance":{"title":"How does this source relate to the theme?","prompt":"A theme matrix should record disagreement, not erase it.","options":{"supports":"The source provides evidence consistent with the theme claim.","conflicts":"The source provides evidence inconsistent with the theme claim under a reasonably comparable question/measure.","mixed":"The source partly supports and partly complicates the theme, or results vary by outcome/subgroup/condition.","background":"The source provides context/method information but is not direct evidence for or against the theme claim."}},"analysisStructure":{"title":"How do I identify the data/design structure?","prompt":"Answer in this order: What is the outcome type? Same units or different units? How many conditions/variables?","options":{"Describe one numerical variable":"One numerical outcome; goal is distribution/center/spread rather than a group comparison.","Describe one categorical variable":"One categorical outcome; summarize counts/proportions.","Association between two measured variables":"Two measured variables from the same observational units; goal is association, not assigned treatment effect.","Two independent groups, numerical outcome":"Different independent units in each of two groups; outcome is numerical.","Two paired/repeated numerical measurements":"The same units (or explicitly matched units) supply two related numerical measurements.","Three or more independent groups":"Different independent units across three or more groups; numerical outcome.","Two categorical variables":"Both variables are categorical and observations belong to appropriate independent/paired structure for the selected method.","Paired binary":"Same units measured in two conditions/times; outcome has exactly two categories.","Repeated binary":"Same units measured across three or more conditions/times; outcome has exactly two categories."},"questions":["Does one unit appear more than once?","Does the outcome have numbers, ordered categories, two categories, or named categories?"]},"outcomeType":{"title":"What kind of outcome do I have?","prompt":"Classify what the recorded values mean, not how they happen to look in the spreadsheet.","options":{"Continuous/numerical":"Meaningful quantities such as height, time, concentration, or score.","Count":"Number of events/items, usually nonnegative integers such as colony count.","Ordinal":"Ordered categories or rankings where equal spacing is not guaranteed.","Binary":"Exactly two categories such as yes/no or correct/incorrect.","Nominal categories":"Named categories with no inherent order, such as species or treatment label.","Text/qualitative":"Open written/spoken/observational evidence analyzed for meaning, patterns, or themes."}},"correlationCoefficient":{"title":"Pearson or Spearman?","prompt":"Do not choose only from a normality test.","options":{"Pearson r":"Describes linear association between two numerical variables; inspect the scatterplot for linear form and influential points.","Spearman \u03c1":"Describes monotonic rank association and is often useful with ordinal data or when a monotonic relationship is more appropriate than a linear one."},"questions":["What does the scatterplot look like?","Are the variables numerical/ordinal and is a linear relationship sensible?"]},"anovaModel":{"title":"Welch or classical one-way ANOVA?","prompt":"Both compare mean structure across independent groups, but their variance assumptions differ.","options":{"Welch ANOVA":"Generally preferable when group variances/sample sizes differ and a one-way independent-group mean comparison is appropriate.","Classical ANOVA":"Uses the classical equal-variance one-way model; use only when that model is justified."}},"fisherChoice":{"title":"Chi-square or Fisher exact in a 2\u00d72 table?","prompt":"Sparse expected counts can make chi-square approximations less reliable.","options":{"Chi-square":"Approximate test for categorical association using cell counts when expected-count conditions are adequate.","Fisher exact":"Exact 2\u00d72 method that is especially useful with sparse counts."}}},"novicePromise":"You are not expected to know research terminology before you begin. When a term or choice appears, the site should explain what it means, how to recognize it in your own project, and what a reasonable example looks like. If a screen still leaves you unsure, use Student Guide or Research Terms before guessing."};
